# Jira

This project's project-management tool. Owns work items (Jira issues), epics, and board/sprint state on **{{SITE}}**, project **{{PROJECT_KEY}}**.

## Owned operations

- **create work item** — create a Jira issue with summary, description, and issue type.
- **transition work item** — move the issue through its workflow (e.g. To Do → In Progress → Done).
- **comment on work item** — add a comment to the issue.
- **set work item field** — Priority, Story Points, issue type, status, or any other project field.
- **link work item** — link the issue to an epic (parent), or to another issue (blocks / relates to / depends on).
- **place work item on board** — ensure the issue appears on project {{PROJECT_KEY}}'s board (by project and status — issues in the project show on its board automatically).
- **create milestone** — create an Epic (epics represent milestones).

Any practice skill that mentions one of these operations routes through this skill.

## Access

All operations go through the **Atlassian MCP server** (Atlassian's official Remote MCP at `https://mcp.atlassian.com/v1/sse`). Do not call the Jira REST API directly.

Authentication is **OAuth 2.1 handled by the editor** — Atlassian's MCP supports dynamic client registration, so the editor runs the consent flow on first use and stores nothing sensitive in the repo. No API token or secret lives in `.mcp.json`. Access follows the authenticated Atlassian account's existing Jira permissions.

### Preflight (first operation of the session)

1. **Server health.** Verify the Atlassian MCP server is connected and authenticated. If it isn't, the operator needs to (re-)run `.agents/scripts/setup/setup-jira.sh` or complete the editor's OAuth flow — halt and say so rather than guessing.
2. **Cloud ID.** Resolve the cloud ID for **{{SITE}}** via `getAccessibleAtlassianResources` (it returns each accessible site's `id` and `url`). Match on the `{{SITE}}` URL and reuse that cloud ID for every subsequent call this session. Halt if {{SITE}} is not in the accessible list.
3. **Project.** Confirm project **{{PROJECT_KEY}}** is visible via `getVisibleJiraProjects` (filter by key). Halt if not.

## Conventions

### Work item titles

The issue **summary** is a plain-English description of the work. **Do not** use conventional commit format (`feat(scope): ...`) — that's for commit messages and PR titles.

### Work item types

Use the project's native Jira issue types. Resolve the available types and their required fields with `getJiraProjectIssueTypesMetadata` / `getJiraIssueTypeMetaWithFields` before creating — projects differ. Typical mapping:

- `Story` — new capabilities or improvements.
- `Bug` — defects and regressions.
- `Epic` — a milestone grouping subordinate stories and bugs.

Do not encode the type in a label.

### Work item fields

Set the project's structured fields — do not put these in the description:

- **Story Points** — the project's estimation field.
- **Priority** — the project's Priority field (e.g. Highest, High, Medium, Low, Lowest).

Field IDs vary per project. Resolve them with `getJiraIssueTypeMetaWithFields` once per session before setting.

### Board

Every work item lives in project **{{PROJECT_KEY}}**, so it appears on that project's board automatically by its status. The complete creation sequence:

#### 1. Create the issue

Create the Jira issue in project `{{PROJECT_KEY}}` with summary, description, and issue type via `createJiraIssue`.

#### 2. Set estimation + priority

Set Story Points and Priority via `editJiraIssue`, using the field IDs resolved from `getJiraIssueTypeMetaWithFields`.

#### 3. Link to its epic (if part of a milestone)

Set the parent/epic link via `editJiraIssue` (parent field) or `createIssueLink`, depending on how the project models the epic relationship.

A work item that has no estimate and no priority set is considered incomplete.

### Work item lifecycle

Transitions are workflow-driven — resolve the valid transitions for an issue with `getTransitionsForJiraIssue` (transition names and IDs differ per workflow), then apply with `transitionJiraIssue`.

- Move to **In Progress** when active implementation starts.
- Move to the post-PR status (often **In Review** or **Ready to Test** — resolve the actual status with `getTransitionsForJiraIssue`) when implementation is complete and a PR is open. The same status survives QA approval.
- **Do not manually move to Done.** PR-merge automation moves the issue to Done when the PR is merged. Manual transitions before merge bypass that gate.

### Milestones (epics)

Epics represent milestones. All subordinate story and bug work items are linked to their parent epic (parent field or epic link, per the project's configuration).

## Composition

When a source-control tool is installed (e.g., `github-source-control`), pull requests link back to work items by referencing the Jira issue key (e.g. `{{PROJECT_KEY}}-123`) in branch names and PR bodies. This skill owns the work-item side of that link — creating the issue, transitioning it, commenting on it — but does **not** own PRs.
